# The multiplicity, the single mechanism that unifies proof erasure, the linear substrate, and uniqueness. Every
# binding carries a quantity drawn from a three-element semiring: zero, one, and many. Zero means used no times at run
# time, which is exactly a proof, present for checking and gone when the program runs. One means used exactly once,
# which is exactly an interaction-net wire and exactly a uniquely owned mutable value, the condition that makes
# in-place reuse sound. Many means used any number of times, an ordinary shared value. One annotation therefore erases
# proofs, tracks the linearity the runtime substrate demands, and licenses safe mutation, all at once. This is
# quantitative type theory, the reason this kernel fits a language whose runtime is literally linear.
#
# The quantities form a semiring under two operations. Addition combines two uses of the same variable: zero is its
# identity, and any sum reaching past one is many. Multiplication composes a use through an enclosing binding: zero
# absorbs, so a use inside an erased binding is erased, and one is its identity. A use fits a binding when its counted
# quantity matches what the binding allows: many accepts anything, one demands exactly one use, zero demands none.
# That fit is the linearity check the type checker runs over every body.

# The quantity on a binding: rank zero is erased, rank one is linear, rank two stands for many. The three ranks are the
# carrier of the semiring; the addition, multiplication, and fit rules above are how the checker reasons over them.
form multiplicity
  link rank, like natural
